Title = {Cysteine-Rich Peptides in Food Systems: Structure, Sources, Functional
   Properties, Potential Applications, and Future Perspectives},
Journal = {PROBIOTICS AND ANTIMICROBIAL PROTEINS},
Year = {2026},
Month = {2026 OCT 14},
Abstract = {Cysteine-rich peptides (CRPs) are structurally diverse bioactive
   peptides in which cysteine residues form disulfide bonds or, in some
   lanthipeptides, cysteine-derived thioether bridges. These constraints
   can improve resistance to thermal, chemical, and enzymatic stress,
   although stability and activity remain peptide- and matrix-dependent.
   CRPs have attracted interest as antimicrobial, antioxidant, and
   immunomodulatory agents in food preservation, functional foods, and
   active packaging. Nisin provides the clearest regulatory and commercial
   precedent: this lanthipeptide is used in selected food categories to
   control susceptible Gram-positive pathogens and spoilage organisms.
   Evidence for most plant defensins, cyclotides, and engineered CRPs,
   however, remains based on in vitro, preclinical, or food-model studies.
   This review examines CRP structures, sources, functional properties,
   potential food applications, and regulatory considerations. It also
   evaluates quantitative efficacy data, manufacturing and delivery
   constraints, allergenicity and toxicity, possible resistance, and the
   need for matrix-specific validation. CRPs should therefore be considered
   candidate components of validated preservation and delivery strategies
   rather than universal replacements for conventional additives.},
